  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">National trade unions confederations in the Philippines jointly presented and have positioned themselves successfully within the design of the Philippine UN Socio-Economic and Peacebuilding Framework (UN SEPF) in May 2021 through the submission of policy proposals for dialogue. The 39 proposals submitted by the unions are now considered and accepted as key inputs in the design of the UN Socio-Economic and Peacebuilding Framework (SEPF).  These will also be considered in the development of the UN Country Teams (UNCT) work-plans.

Furthermore, during the successful engagement with the UN Resident Coordinators Office and the greater UN Country Team on May 28, 2021 and June 8, 2021, respectively led to assurances and commitment from the wider UN to regularise the trade union consultation process with the wider UN and inclusion of unions within the joint planning, activity construction and monitoring of the UNSDCF. The ILO supported the national trade union confederations in the Philippines to conduct a series of meetings  to prepare the unions for a more systematic  engagement on the UN process in the Philippines including the Common Country Assessment (CCA), and the formulation of the Socio-Economic and Peacebuilding Framework (UN SEPF), as well as follow up briefings (October 2020). 

Relevant documents and guides were shared including the ILO publication &quot;United Nations Sustainable Development Cooperation and the Decent Work Agenda &#x2013; A Trade Union Reference Manual&quot; as part of the technical material with the trade union confederations in December 2020. 

To consolidate the positions of the various trade union confederations, the ILO engaged the work of a consultant to work with the unions to produce a workers&apos; perspectives and analysis on the SEPF (from December 2020 to February 2021).    Leaders of 14 national trade unions, labour and civil society organizations, as well as  sectoral representatives of four councils under of the National Anti-Poverty Commission  (NAPC), participated in the collaborative and consultative process in coming up with a joint position paper. 

The trade union participants in this process, made significant recommendations for many of the focus areas and priorities for each of the three pillars of the UNSEPF.

The ILO coordinated and supported as liaison point between the UNRCO and the greater UN system in the Philippines, by coordinating the required meetings for engagement with the trade unions in May and June of 2021.</narrative>
   </description>
